  "notes": "Brooklyn psychedelic-horror rap, entirely in-house produced, and the research puts it at the *consonant* end of this axis: \"more harmonic movement than the trap-metal branch\", with string beds and detuned pads giving m7/m9/add9 colours over i–VI–III–VII and i–iv–VII. So this is the only entry in this batch with four-chord families, `maxChords` 4 and `extensions` weighted away from triads. Like Danny Brown it is not a half-time trap grid: the documented default is 80–100 straight with the snare on 2 and 4, so `halfTime` is off and `snare.placement` is `backbeat_24`, with the range reaching 150 for the trap-leaning cuts. The most specific drum claim is a mix one that is still a MIDI fact — the \"hard-hitting\" quality comes from parallel-compressed drum buses rather than clipping — which is authored as a *narrowed* dynamic range: accents at 104–120 over mains at 92–104 and ghosts that never drop below 58, so nothing is quiet and nothing is at the ceiling. Hats are 8ths or a ride, so `base` is `8th` with `ride` in `percs.lanes`. The three-voice arrangement (loop + drone + counter-line) is why `melody.densityPerBar` reaches 9 and `countermelody.densityRatio` is 0.6 with a `sustain_pad` alongside the two required styles — the research says note density here is above lane average. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium; the INFERRED parameters are the tempi, which the paragraph says are inferred, and the specific `velocityTiers` numbers, which are my reading of \"parallel compression\" rather than a documented figure.",
